  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1246 AM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nModels remain in good agreement through the end of the work \nweek, with differences by the weekend. For the most part, the \nupper pattern will favor high pressure to our southeast, and an \nupper low over the Pacific Northwest with persistent southwest \nflow across Colorado.\n\nTuesday...a cold front will have arrived by Tuesday morning\nacross the Plains, with low level flow turning northeasterly\nthrough the morning, and southeasterly by the afternoon. This\nwill help keep moisture pooled along the higher terrain, and\nweak instability is expected by the afternoon. Showers and\nthunderstorms will develop initially across the higher terrain,\nand spread east int o the Plains during the evening hours. PWAT\nvalues increase slightly on Tuesday, with stronger storms able\nto produce heavier rainfall, with an elevated flash flood risk\non area burn scars. Lightning and outflow wind gusts in excess\nof 40 mph will also be possible. Overall, shower and \nthunderstorm coverage will be increasing on Tuesday across the \nMountains, and weaken as they move out into the Plains due to a \nlack of instability. Temperatures will be much cooler behind the\nfront, with most areas in the 70s for highs!\n\nWednesday through Friday...flow turns more southerly aloft as\nthe upper trough drop south along the West Coast. PWAT values\ncontinue to increase, with values in excess of an inch forecast\nacross the Plains by Wednesday, and prevailing into Friday.\nMultiple upper waves embedded in the flow will lift across\nColorado, with several rounds of heavier showers and\nthunderstorms expected. Flash flooding potential on area burn\nscars will increase, especially Wednesday and Thursday.\nTemperatures will warm slightly, with mostly 70s and 80s across\nthe Plains. By Friday, the upper trough lifts across the\nNorthern Rockies with one last good day of shower and\nthunderstorm activity across the area.\n\nSaturday through Monday...the upper trough continues into the\nGreat Lakes through the weekend, with upper level ridging\nmoving across Utah and into Colorado. Drier air will move into\nthe region with the upper ridging with shower and thunderstorm\nactivity decreasing, to just isolated to scattered activity over\nthe Mountains by Sunday and Monday. It should be noted, that\nmodel differences arise by next weekend, with lower confidence\nin the overall pattern. Temperatures will remain nearly steady\nwith 70s and 80s across the lower elevations.",
